The Micronix cold boot set

These are the intact copies, from

    ~/src-orig/src-local/cpnet/server/rlee/M/MORROW/MICRONIX/COLDBOOT

which is one of five identical copies on this machine - the others are
under archives/amaus, and rlee's BACKUPS/8INCH/D8, DEVELOP/BOX-10 and
LOOSE DISKS/022.  README.orig here is that disk's own README, and it is
the authority for what follows.  Bitsavers has a second, older version
under Morrow/micronix/1.3/1008-8; it differs from these only in the 165
bytes at 0x800 to 0x94c, and not at all in the second stage.

They were also on disks/floppies/Micronix1.6/cold_boot_loader.bin, and
can be pulled off it with the diskdefs here:

    cpmls -f morrow5 cold_boot_loader.bin
    cpmcp -f morrow5 cold_boot_loader.bin 0:M5LOAD ./M5LOAD

but do not - that image is damaged.  M5LOAD off it has content only in
bytes 2048 to 7167 and DJLOAD only 8192 to 9215, the rest being E5, which
is what an unwritten CP/M block reads as.  The copies here have 74 and 69
stray E5 bytes respectively, which is just data.

What they are, in the disk's own words:

    djboot.com   Boot Micronix from a 5 or 8 inch floppy.
    m5boot.com   Boot Micronix from an M5  drive with the HDDMA controller.
    m10boot.com  Boot Micronix from an M10 drive with the HDDMA controller.
    m16boot.com  Boot Micronix from an M16 drive with the HDDMA controller.

    djload, m5load, m10load, m16load - "versions of the above bootstrap
    programs suitable for loading onto the system tracks of a 5 inch
    floppy disk.  For example, if you do a sysgen m10load to a 5 inch
    floppy, then as long as you have that floppy in drive A, pressing
    RESET will boot Micronix from the M10."

So there are two ways in, and neither of them puts anything on the hard
disk.  Either you are in CP/M and run m5boot.com, or you sysgen m5load
onto a floppy's system tracks and RESET boots that floppy, which then
boots Micronix off the hard disk.  This is why m5init and hdinit never
write a boot block - there is nothing to write.  The hard disk holds a
filesystem and nothing else.

The xxLOAD layout is the same in all of them, and is a floppy's system
tracks rather than anything to do with a hard disk:

    0000-07ff   zero
    0800        first level boot, 128 bytes, runs at 0080
    0a00        second level, the crt0 "ld sp,0100 / call ... / jp 0"
                and then "Drive not ready", "Micronix loader for ..."

DJLOAD's second level is the loader in micronix16-loader.bin, found at
0a00, which is what says these files are images of the tracks that loader
was read off.  The first level code is common to all of them - M5LOAD's
first 92 bytes at 0800 are the floppy's exactly, and 92 is 5c, which is
where the channel program starts.  Same code, different channel program,
which is the only part that has to know what it is talking to.
